advapi32: CredEnumerate() no longer fails when the macOS Keychain has no match for the filter.

CredEnumerateW() collects credentials from the registry, then from the host's store through
mountmgr. On macOS that store is the Keychain, and mountmgr's search_credentials() returns
STATUS_NOT_FOUND when no Keychain item matches the filter. CredEnumerateW() only forgave
ERROR_NOT_SUPPORTED (the answer on Linux, where there is no host store), so on macOS a filter that
matched registry credentials but no Keychain item failed the whole call with ERROR_NOT_FOUND and the
registry matches were thrown away. Upstream Wine has the same two lines today.

A program that keeps its own generic credentials and looks them up by prefix never finds them.
The Xbox Live sign-in library does exactly that: it reads its device key with
CredEnumerateW(L"Xbl|DeviceKey*") and its tokens with CredEnumerateW(L"Xbl|<title>|...*"). Measured
on an M4 (macOS 27.0, x64-crossover26.3 r17 + 0017 + 0018) with Forza Horizon 6: the bottle held
thirteen "Xbl..." generic credentials (an unfiltered CredEnumerateW listed them), every filtered
call returned ERROR_NOT_FOUND (1168), and on each launch the game generated a new device key
(BCryptGenerateKeyPair straight after the failed lookups, then CredWriteW of "Xbl|DeviceKey") and
asked the player to sign in to Xbox again with a device code, six times in one evening
(2026-10-05).

An empty answer from the host is now treated like an unsupported host: the count from the
registry decides, and ERROR_NOT_FOUND is still returned when nothing matched anywhere.

--- a/dlls/advapi32/cred.c
+++ b/dlls/advapi32/cred.c
@@ -1059,7 +1059,8 @@
     if (ret == ERROR_SUCCESS)
     {
         ret = host_enumerate_credentials(Filter, NULL, NULL, &len, Count);
-        if (ret == ERROR_NOT_SUPPORTED) ret = ERROR_SUCCESS;
+        /* nothing matching in the host's store is not a failure, the registry may hold the matches */
+        if (ret == ERROR_NOT_SUPPORTED || ret == ERROR_NOT_FOUND) ret = ERROR_SUCCESS;
     }
     if (ret == ERROR_SUCCESS && *Count == 0)
         ret = ERROR_NOT_FOUND;
@@ -1085,7 +1086,7 @@
             if (ret == ERROR_SUCCESS)
             {
                 ret = host_enumerate_credentials(Filter, *Credentials, buffer, &len, Count);
-                if (ret == ERROR_NOT_SUPPORTED) ret = ERROR_SUCCESS;
+                if (ret == ERROR_NOT_SUPPORTED || ret == ERROR_NOT_FOUND) ret = ERROR_SUCCESS;
             }
         }
         else ret = ERROR_OUTOFMEMORY;
